        {
          "dimension": "Execution / delivery slippage",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "Multi-phase plans like MakerDAO's five-phase Endgame and Aave V4's staged testnet-to-mainnet sequence expose protocols to compounding delay risk where each missed milestone erodes community confidence in subsequent phases.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Centralization / founder dependency",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "The click premium on Zeus's OHM return and Sam Kazemian's personal Singularity presentations reveals that many DeFi roadmaps are effectively single-founder commitments with no formal binding mechanism — departure or pivot voids the plan.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Regulatory timing mismatch",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "G20 and Bank Negara Malaysia crypto roadmaps impose external compliance deadlines that may force protocol pivots mid-execution, particularly for stablecoin and RWA-adjacent projects like Frax and MakerDAO.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Liquidity fragmentation during transition",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Protocols launching new chains or L3s (Fraxtal, Unichain) risk fragmenting existing liquidity pools before the new environment achieves sufficient depth, creating a window where neither the old nor new venue is optimally liquid.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Smart contract / upgrade risk",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Ethereum hard fork roadmaps (Prague EIPs, L1 Strawmap proposals) introduce EVM-level changes that propagate risk across every protocol built on the base layer, with the 2024 Cancun upgrade demonstrating that even well-planned forks require extensive downstream auditing.",
          "source": "https://ethereum.org/roadmap/"
        },
